# Explore Dark Existence

Begin with the central distinction: a deductive system may establish that some object satisfies a property while establishing the property for none of the objects on a chosen naming list. This is **dark existence**, a statement about provability and naming rather than ordinary search difficulty.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

The diagnostic above exposes the key boundary. If existential proofs always provide a provable named witness, darkness is impossible. If no such extraction principle is available, the definition is not immediately contradictory.

## The arithmetic of finite tags

A positive number $r$ of tags is interleaved over the old names by quotient and remainder: $c=rn+i$, where $i=c mod r$ and $n=\lfloor c/rfloor$. Explore this bijective bookkeeping directly.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

For background on the arithmetic used here, see [Euclidean division](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_division). The grid visualization makes the same coding visible at a glance.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the full coverage argument</summary>
For every tag $i$ with $0\le i<r$ and every old name index $n$, choose the code $c=rn+i$. Euclidean division returns remainder $i$ and quotient $n$, so the new naming map reaches $(i,
u(n))$. Conversely, every code decodes to exactly one such pair.
</details>

## Amplifying one witness

The tagged predicate $P^{[r]}(i,x)$ means simply $P(x)$. If $x$ is one witness, then $(0,x),\ldots,(r-1,x)$ are $r$ distinct pairs. Their tags differ, but their mathematical payload does not.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the Finite-Tag Amplification Theorem</summary>
Assume $P$ is dark: the system proves $\exists x\,P(x)$ but proves no $P(
u(n))$. Assume also that proofs can be transformed through finite tagging. The existential proof yields a proof of at least $r$ tagged witnesses. If a tagged named instance at code $c$ were provable, erasing its irrelevant tag would give the forbidden old instance $P(
u(\lfloor c/rfloor))$. Hence the tagged predicate is dark at level $r$.
</details>

## Why raw levels are not intrinsic

Witness count can rise to any positive finite value through tags while underlying content stays fixed. It can also move downward: from an $n$-element witness set, select an $m$-element subset whenever $m\le n$.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the hierarchy obstruction</summary>
A rank invariant under irrelevant finite tags must assign the same value to $P$ and $P^{[r]}$. A rank that strictly follows raw witness level would assign them different values. No rank can satisfy both requirements, so predicates must first be quotiented by harmless representation changes.
</details>

## What remains open

Famous arithmetic independence principles are not automatic examples: their standard forms are global universal or termination claims, while dark existence requires a provable existential and a separate nonprovability result for every named instance. Likewise, “density” among formulas has no meaning until a topology or measure robust under recoding is fixed. For general background, consult resources on [Peano arithmetic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peano_axioms), the [Paris–Harrington theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris%E2%80%93Harrington_theorem), and [Gödel numbering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6del_numbering).

